==Logotype==
In 1972, the bank created the blue logo "Slash in a Square" – designed by [[Anton Stankowski]] and intended to represent growth within a risk-controlled framework.<ref>{{cite web |title=Deutsche Bank Logo: Design and History |url=http://www.famouslogos.net/deutsche-bank-logo/ |website=famouslogos.net |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121018004004/http://www.famouslogos.net/deutsche-bank-logo |archive-date=18 October 2012 |access-date=25 May 2026}}</ref>

==Business divisions==
[[File:Deutsche Bank building - Sydney.jpg|thumb|180px|Deutsche Bank, [[Sydney]]]]
The bank's business model rests on three pillars – the Corporate & Investment Bank (CIB), the Private & Commercial Bank and Asset Management (DWS).

===Corporate and Investment Bank (CIB)===
[[File:Deutsche Bank munich.jpg|thumb|A Deutsche Bank retail branch in [[Munich]]]]
The Corporate & Investment Bank (CIB) is Deutsche Bank's capital markets business. The CIB comprises the below six units.<ref>{{cite web |title=Welcome to the Investment Bank |url=https://www.db.com/company/en/corporate-and-investment-bank.htm |website=db.com |publisher=Deutsche Bank |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

* '''Corporate Finance''' is responsible for advisory and mergers & acquisitions (M&A).
* '''Equities / Fixed Income & Currencies'''. These two units are responsible for sales and trading of securities.
* '''Global Capital Markets''' (GCM) is focused on financing and risk management solutions. It includes debt and equity issuances.
* '''Global Transaction Banking''' (GTB) caters to corporates and financial institutions by providing commercial banking products including cross-border payments, cash management, [[Securities Services]], and international trade finance.
* '''Deutsche Bank Research''' provides analysis of products, markets, and trading strategies.

===Private and Commercial Bank===

Private Bank is the retail and private banking division of Deutsche Bank. In Germany, it operates under two brands – Deutsche Bank and Postbank. Additionally, the division maintains operations in Belgium, Italy, Spain, and India. The business units in Poland and Portugal were divested in 2018 and 2019, respectively.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shotter |first=James |date=14 December 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank sells chunk of Polish business to Santander |url=https://www.ft.com/content/a40e508a-e0ad-11e7-8f9f-de1c2175f5ce |website=[[Financial Times]] |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221210/https://www.ft.com/content/a40e508a-e0ad-11e7-8f9f-de1c2175f5ce |archive-date=10 December 2022 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=27 March 2018 |title=Deutsche Bank To Sell Portuguese Private and Commercial Clients Business To Abanca |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/brief-deutsche-bank-to-sell-portuguese-p/brief-deutsche-bank-to-sell-portuguese-private-commercial-clients-business-to-abanca-idUSFWN1R905B |website=[[Reuters]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=The Financial |date=28 May 2018 |title=Germany's biggest Private & Commercial Bank is launched |url=https://www.finchannel.com/~finchannel/business/banksandservices/73669-germany-s-biggest-private-commercial-bank-is-launched |website=finchannel.com |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016165534/https://www.finchannel.com/~finchannel/business/banksandservices/73669-germany-s-biggest-private-commercial-bank-is-launched |archive-date=16 October 2018 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=9 November 2018 |title=Deutsche Bank completes demerger of its Polish Private and Commercial Banking business to Santander Bank Polska |url=https://www.db.com/news/detail/20181109-deutsche-bank-completes-demerger-of-its-polish-private-and-commercial-banking-business-to-santander-bank-polska?language_id=1 |website=db.com |publisher=Deutsche Bank |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=14 May 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank completes sale of retail business to Abanca |url=https://www.theportugalnews.com/news/deutsche-bank-completes-sale-of-retail-business-to-abanca/49881 |website=theportugalnews.com |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>
* '''Wealth Management''' functions as the bank's private banking arm, serving [[high-net-worth individual]]s and families worldwide. The division has a presence in the world's private banking hotspots, including Switzerland, Luxembourg, the Channel Islands, the Cayman Islands and Dubai.<ref>{{cite web |title=Wealth Management – Locations |url=https://deutschewealth.com/content/deutschewealth/en/locations.html |website=deutschewealth.com |publisher=Deutsche Bank |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

===Deutsche Asset Management (DWS)===
Deutsche Bank holds a majority stake in the listed asset manager [[DWS Group]] (formerly Deutsche Asset Management), which was separated from the bank in March 2018.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Osman |first1=Yasmin |last2=Rezmer |first2=Anke |date=5 December 2017 |title=Überraschung vor dem Börsengang |url=https://www.handelsblatt.com/finanzen/banken-versicherungen/banken/deutsche-asset-management-ueberraschung-vor-dem-boersengang/20670532.html |website=[[Handelsblatt]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

==Controversies==

Deutsche Bank in general as well as specific employees have frequently figured in controversies and allegations of deceitful behavior or illegal transactions. As of 2016, the bank was involved in some 7,800 legal disputes and calculated €5.4&nbsp;billion as litigation reserves,<ref>{{cite web |date=24 May 2016 |title=Deutsche Bank: Zwischen Ramsch und Skandalen |url=http://boerse.ard.de/aktien/deutsche-bank-zwischen-ramsch-und-skandalen100.html |website=boerse.ard.de |publisher=[[ARD (broadcaster)|ARD]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170404093959/http://boerse.ard.de/aktien/deutsche-bank-zwischen-ramsch-und-skandalen100.html |archive-date=4 April 2017 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> with a further €2.2&nbsp;billion held against other contingent liabilities.<ref name="Euromoney" /> According to the ''New Yorker'', Deutsche Bank has long had an "abject" reputation.<ref name="The New Yorker-2020" /> Between 2008 and 2016, Deutsche Bank paid around nine billion dollars in fines and settlements related to wrongdoings across different issue areas.<ref name="The New Yorker-2020" /> The [[FinCEN Files|FinCEN file]] leaks documented around $1.3&nbsp;trillion of suspicious transactions through Deutsche Bank between 1999 and 2017.<ref name="The New Yorker-2020" /> More than half of all suspicious transactions involving major banks in the FinCEN files leaks involved Deutsche Bank.<ref name="The New Yorker-2020" />

=== Banking for Donald Trump, 1995–2021===
{{anchor|Robert Mueller investigation}}
{{further|Timeline of investigations into Trump and Russia (January–June 2017)|Timeline of investigations into Trump and Russia (July–December 2017)|Timeline of investigations into Trump and Russia (2019)|Wealth of Donald Trump}}

Deutsche Bank is widely recognized as being the largest creditor to real-estate mogul and politician [[Donald Trump]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |date=4 February 2020 |title=The Money Behind Trump's Money |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/04/magazine/deutsche-bank-trump.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Harding |first1=Luke |last2=Makortoff |first2=Kalyeena |date=13 January 2021 |title=Deutsche Bank joins companies cutting ties with Donald Trump |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jan/12/deutsche-bank-severs-ties-with-donald-trump |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=12 January 2021 |title=Trump dropped by biggest lender Deutsche Bank for future business: NYT |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trump-deutsche-bank-idUSKBN29H0PM |work=[[Reuters]] |language=en |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> 45th President of the United States, lending him and his company more than $2&nbsp;billion over twenty years ending 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Enrich |first1=David |last2=Protess |first2=Ben |last3=Rashbaum |first3=William K. |last4=Weiser |first4=Benjamin |date=5 August 2020 |title=Trump's Bank Was Subpoenaed by N.Y. Prosecutors in Criminal Inquiry |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/05/nyregion/trump-taxes-vance-deutsche-bank.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> The bank held more than $360 million in outstanding loans to him prior to his 2016 election. Although his 2019 final report never mentioned Deutsche Bank, as of December 2017, [[Mueller special counsel investigation|Special Counsel Robert Mueller]] investigated Deutsche Bank's role in Trump and Russian parties allegedly cooperating to elect him.<ref name="busin-2018">{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Allan |date=8 December 2017 |title=Trump's long and winding history with Deutsche Bank could now be at the center of Robert Mueller's investigation |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/trump-deutsche-bank-mueller-2017-12 |website=businessinsider.com |publisher=[[Business Insider]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> {{As of|2019|March}}, Deutsche Bank's relationship with Trump was also under investigation by two [[United States congressional committee|U.S. congressional committees]] and [[New York civil investigation of The Trump Organization|by the New York attorney general]].<ref name="2019-03-18-nyt">{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |author-link=David Enrich |date=18 March 2019 |title=A Mar-a-Lago Weekend and an Act of God: Trump's History With Deutsche Bank |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/03/18/business/trump-deutsche-bank.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |author-link=David Enrich |date=18 March 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank and Trump: $2 Billion in Loans and a Wary Board |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/03/18/business/deutsche-bank-donald-trump.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="TrakingTrumpInvestigationsNYT">{{cite news |last1=Buchanan |first1=Larry |last2=Yourish |first2=Karen |date=25 September 2019 |title=Tracking 30 Investigations Related to Trump |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2019/05/13/us/politics/trump-investigations.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

In April 2019, [[United States House of Representatives|House]] [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] subpoenaed the Bank for Trump's personal and financial records.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Flitter |first1=Emily |last2=Enrich |first2=David |author-link2=David Enrich |date=15 April 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank Is Subpoenaed for Trump Records by House Democrats |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/15/business/deutsche-bank-trump-finances-congress.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Scannell |first1=Kara |last2=Herb |first2=Jeremy |date=15 April 2019 |title=House committees issue subpoenas to Deutsche Bank, JP Morgan Chase, Citigroup, Bank of America to probe Trump's finances |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/04/15/politics/deutsche-bank-subpoena/index.html |website=[[CNN]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Warmbrodt |first1=Zachary |last2=Bresnahan |first2=John |date=15 April 2019 |title=House Democrats subpoena Deutsche Bank in Trump investigation |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2019/04/15/democrats-subpoena-deutsche-bank-1277199 |website=[[Politico]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hosenball |first1=Mark |author-link1=Mark Hosenball |last2=Beech |first2=Eric |last3=Zargham |first3=Mohammad |last4=Osterman |first4=Cynthia |last5=Adler |first5=Leslie |date=15 April 2019 |title=House panels issue subpoenas to Deutsche Bank, others in Trump probe |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trump-russia-banks-idUSKCN1RR2FW |work=[[Reuters]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Demirjian |first=Karoun |author-link=Karoun Demirjian |date=15 April 2019 |title=House Democrats subpoena Deutsche Bank, other financial institutions tied to Trump |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/house-democrats-subpoena-deutsche-bank-other-financial-institutions-tied-to-trump/2019/04/15/00d0042e-5fee-11e9-9ff2-abc984dc9eec_story.html |work=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> On 29 April 2019, U.S. President Donald Trump, his business, and his children [[Donald Trump Jr.]], [[Eric Trump]], and [[Ivanka Trump]] sued Deutsche Bank and [[Capital One]] bank to block them from turning over financial records to congressional committees.<ref>{{cite news |last=Alesci |first=Cristina |author-link=Cristina Alesci |date=30 April 2019 |title=Trump team sues Deutsche Bank and Capital One to keep them from turning over financial records to Congress |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/04/29/politics/trump-lawsuit-capital-one/index.html |website=[[CNN]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> On 22 May 2019, judge [[Edgardo Ramos]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York|federal District Court in Manhattan]] rejected the Trump suit against Deutsche Bank, ruling the bank must comply with congressional subpoenas.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Flitter |first1=Emily |last2=McKinley |first2=Jesse |last3=Enrich |first3=David |author-link3=David Enrich |last4=Fandos |first4=Nicholas |date=22 May 2019 |title=Trump's Financial Secrets Move Closer to Disclosure |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/22/business/deutsche-bank-trump-subpoena.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> Six days later, Ramos granted Trump's attorneys their request for a [[Stay of proceedings|stay]] so they could pursue an expedited appeal through the courts.<ref>{{cite news |last=Merle |first=Renae |date=28 May 2019 |title=House subpoenas for Trump's bank records put on hold while president appeals |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2019/05/29/house-subpoenas-trumps-bank-records-put-hold-while-president-appeals/ |work=[[The Washington Post]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> In October 2019, a federal appeals court said the bank asserted it did not have Trump's tax returns.<ref>{{cite news |last=Scuffham |first=Matt |author-link=Matt Scuffham |date=11 October 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank tells U.S. court it does not have Trump's tax returns |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trump-tax-deutsche-bank-idUSKBN1WP28E |work=[[Reuters]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> In December 2019, the [[Second Circuit Court of Appeals]] ruled that Deutsche Bank must release Trump's financial records, with some exceptions, to congressional committees; Trump was given seven days to seek another stay pending a possible appeal to the Supreme Court.<ref>{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |author-link=David Enrich |date=3 December 2019 |title=Trump Loses Appeal on Deutsche Bank Subpoenas |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/03/business/trump-deutsche-bank-subpoena.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

In May 2019, ''[[The New York Times]]'' reported that anti-money laundering specialists in the bank detected what appeared to be suspicious transactions involving entities controlled by Trump and his son-in-law [[Jared Kushner]], for which they recommended filing [[suspicious activity report]]s with the [[Financial Crimes Enforcement Network]] of the [[United States Department of the Treasury|Treasury Department]], but bank executives rejected the recommendations. One specialist noted money moving from [[Kushner Companies]] to Russian individuals and flagged it in part because of the bank's previous involvement in a Russian money-laundering scheme.<ref>{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |author-link=David Enrich |date=19 May 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank Staff Saw Suspicious Activity in Trump and Kushner Accounts |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/19/business/deutsche-bank-trump-kushner.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Mullen |first=Jethro |date=31 January 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank fined for $10 billion Russian money-laundering scheme |url=https://money.cnn.com/2017/01/31/investing/deutsche-bank-us-fine-russia-money-laundering/index.html |website=[[CNN]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170131082341/http://money.cnn.com/2017/01/31/investing/deutsche-bank-us-fine-russia-money-laundering/index.html |archive-date=31 January 2017 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

On 19 November 2019, Thomas Bowers, a former Deutsche Bank executive and head of the American wealth management division, was reported to have committed suicide in his Malibu home.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Levai |first1=Eric |last2=Stedman |first2=Scott |date=27 November 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank Executive Who Signed Off On Trump Loans Kills Himself At Age 55 |url=https://forensicnews.net/2019/11/27/deutsche-bank-executive-who-signed-off-on-trump-loans-kills-self-at-age-55/ |website=[[Forensic News]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191127190450/https://forensicnews.net/2019/11/27/deutsche-bank-executive-who-signed-off-on-trump-loans-kills-self-at-age-55/ |archive-date=27 November 2019 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> Bowers had been in charge of overseeing and personally signing over $360 million in high-risk loans for Trump's National Doral Miami resort.<ref>Flitter, Emily; Enrich, David (15 April 2019). "Deutsche Bank Is Subpoenaed for Trump Records by House Democrats". ''The New York Times''. Retrieved 16 April 2019.</ref> The loans had been subject to a criminal investigation by special counsel Robert Mueller in his investigation of the president's 2016 campaign involvement in Russian election meddling. Documents on those loans have also been subpoenaed from Deutsche Bank by the House Democrats together with the financial documents of the president. A relationship between Bowers's responsibilities and apparent suicide has not been established; the Los Angeles County Medical Examiner – Coroner closed the case, giving no indication to wrongdoing by third parties.<ref>{{cite web |title=Los Angeles County Medical Examiner-Coroner |url=https://mec.lacounty.gov/case-detail/?caseNumber=2019-08698 |website=[[County of Los Angeles Medical Examiner]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191127183008/https://mec.lacounty.gov/case-detail/?caseNumber=2019-08698 |archive-date=27 November 2019 |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref> In early 2021, Deutsche Bank elected to discontinue its relationship with Trump following the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Riley |first1=Charles |last2=Egan |first2=Matt |date=12 January 2021 |title=Deutsche Bank won't do any more business with Trump |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/04/15/politics/deutsche-bank-subpoena/index.html |website=[[CNN]] |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

===Role in the 2008 financial crisis===
{{see also|2008 financial crisis}}
In January 2017, Deutsche Bank agreed to a $7.2&nbsp;billion settlement with the [[United States Department of Justice]] over its sale and pooling of toxic mortgage securities in the years leading up to the [[2008 financial crisis]]. As part of the agreement, Deutsche Bank was required to pay a civil monetary penalty of $3.1&nbsp;billion and provide $4.1&nbsp;billion in consumer relief, such as loan forgiveness. At the time of the agreement, Deutsche Bank was still facing investigations into the alleged manipulation of foreign exchange rates, suspicious equities trades in Russia, as well as alleged violations of [[United States sanctions against Iran]] and other countries. Since 2012, Deutsche Bank had paid more than €12&nbsp;billion for litigation, including a deal with U.S. mortgage-finance giants [[Fannie Mae]] and [[Freddie Mac]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Freifeld |first1=Karen |last2=Schuetze |first2=Arno |last3=Jones |first3=Kathrin |date=23 December 2016 |title=Deutsche Bank agrees to $7.2 billion mortgage settlement with U.S. |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-deutsche-bank-mortgages-settlement-idUSKBN14C041 |work=[[Reuters]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=26 May 2026}}</ref>

===Espionage scandal, 2009===
In 2009, the bank admitted it engaged in covert espionage on its critics from 2001 to 2007 directed by its corporate security department, although it characterized the incidents as "isolated".<ref name="WSJ">{{cite news |last1=Crawford |first1=David |last2=Karnitschnig |first2=Matthew |date=3 August 2009 |title=Bank Spy Scandal Widens |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB124925060899599929 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |access-date=27 May 2026 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> According to ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'', Deutsche Bank had prepared a list of names of people who it wanted investigated for criticism of the bank, including [[Michael Bohndorf]] (an activist investor in the bank), [[Leo Kirch]] (a former media executive in litigation with the bank), and the Munich law firm of [[Bub Gauweiler & Partner]], which represented Kirch.<ref name=WSJ /> According to the ''Wall Street Journal'', the bank's legal department was involved in the scheme along with its corporate security department.<ref name=WSJ /> The bank hired [[Cleary Gottlieb Steen & Hamilton]] to investigate the incidents on its behalf. The Cleary firm submitted its report, which however was not made public.<ref name=WSJ /> According to ''The Wall Street Journal'', the Cleary firm uncovered a plan by which Deutsche Bank was to infiltrate the Bub Gauweiler firm by having a bank [[Mole (espionage)|mole]] hired as an intern at the Bub Gauweiler firm. The plan was allegedly cancelled after the intern was hired but before she started work.<ref name=WSJ /> [[Peter Gauweiler]], a principal at the targeted law firm, was quoted as saying "I expect the appropriate authorities including state prosecutors and the bank's oversight agencies will conduct a full investigation."<ref name=WSJ />

Deutsche Bank's law firm Cleary Gottlieb Steen & Hamilton in Frankfurt<ref>{{cite press release |date=22 May 2009 |title=Deutsche Bank undertakes independent investigation |url=http://www.deutsche-bank.de/presse/en/content/press_releases_2009_4505.htm |publisher=Deutsche Bank |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100601092546/http://www.deutsche-bank.de/presse/en/content/press_releases_2009_4505.htm |archive-date=1 June 2010 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> published a report in July 2009 saying,<ref name="press">{{cite press release |date=22 July 2009 |title=Deutsche Bank gives update on inquiries |url=http://www.deutsche-bank.de/presse/en/content/press_releases_2009_4551.htm |publisher=Deutsche Bank |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100601075226/http://www.deutsche-bank.de/presse/en/content/press_releases_2009_4551.htm |archive-date=1 June 2010 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> it found no systemic misbehaviour and there was no indication that present members of the management board had been involved in any activity that raises legal issues or has had any knowledge of such activities.<ref name=press/> This was confirmed by the Public Prosecutor's Office in Frankfurt in October 2009.<ref>{{cite press release |date=8 October 2009 |title=Press release Public Prosecutor's Office in Frankfurt |url=http://www.sta-frankfurt.justiz.hessen.de/irj/STA_Frankfurt_am_Main_Internet?rid=HMdJ_15/STA_Frankfurt_am_Main_Internet/sub/3e3/3e3300ea-27aa-2421-f012-f31e2389e481,,,11111111-2222-3333-4444-100000005003&overview=true.htm |publisher=Public Prosecutor's Office in Frankfurt |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120512201033/http://www.sta-frankfurt.justiz.hessen.de/irj/STA_Frankfurt_am_Main_Internet?rid=HMdJ_15%2FSTA_Frankfurt_am_Main_Internet%2Fsub%2F3e3%2F3e3300ea-27aa-2421-f012-f31e2389e481%2C%2C%2C11111111-2222-3333-4444-100000005003&overview=true.htm |archive-date=12 May 2012 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> BaFin found deficiencies in operations within Deutsche Bank's security unit in Germany but no systemic misconduct.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crawford |first1=David |last2=Nissen |first2=Madeleine |date=18 December 2009 |title=Deutsche Bank Probe Finds Individual Misconduct |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704238104574601913577800846 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> The bank said it took steps to strengthen controls for the mandating of external service providers by its Corporate Security Department.<ref name=press/>

===Deutsche Bank document release, 2014===
On 26 January 2014, William S. Broeksmit, a risk specialist at Deutsche Bank who was very close to Anshu Jain and hired by Edson Mitchell to spearhead Deutsche Bank's foray into international investments and money management in the 1990s, released numerous Deutsche Bank documents from the New York branch of the Deutsche Bank Trust Company Americas (DBTCA), which Broeksmit's adopted son [[Val Broeksmit]], who is a close friend of [[Moby]], later gave, along with numerous emails, to both [[Welt am Sonntag]] and [[ZDF]], which revealed numerous irregularities including both a $10&nbsp;billion money laundering scheme spearheaded by the Russia branch of Deutsche Bank at Moscow, which the New York State Department of Financial Services fined Deutsche Bank $425&nbsp;million, and derivatives improprieties.<ref>{{cite news |date=14 May 2017 |title=Was suicide of Deutsche Bank executive linked to Trump and Russia money-laundering? |url=https://www.dailykos.com/story/2017/5/14/1662227/-Was-suicide-of-Deutsche-Bank-executive-linked-to-Trump-and-Russia-money-laundering |work=[[Daily Kos]] |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Aust |first1=Stefan |last2=Laabs |first2=Dirk |date=14 May 2017 |title=Manager-Selbstmord: Um seine Leiche herum lagen interne Dokumente der Deutschen Bank |url=https://www.welt.de/wirtschaft/article164534406/Um-seine-Leiche-herum-lagen-interne-Dokumente-der-Deutschen-Bank.html |work=[[Die Welt]] |language=de |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="nytimes.com"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Spiegelman |first=Ian |date=27 November 2019 |title=Former Deutsche Bank Exec Connected to Trump Loans Dies by Suicide in Malibu |url=https://www.lamag.com/citythinkblog/deutsche-bank-death/ |work=[[Los Angeles Magazine]] |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Ginsberg |first=Merle |date=2 October 2019 |title=How Moby Reportedly Played a Role in an Unfolding Trump-Whistleblower Story |url=https://www.lamag.com/citythinkblog/moby-trump-whistleblower/ |work=[[Los Angeles Magazine]] |language=en-US |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="Me and My Whistle-Blower">{{cite news |last=Enrich |first=David |date=1 October 2019 |title=Me and My Whistle-Blower |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/01/business/val-broeksmit-deutsche-bank-trump-whistle-blower.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="Enrich-2019b"/>{{efn|Val Broeksmit (b. 1976, [[Ukraine]]) emigrated to Chicago in 1979 with his parents Alexander and Alla. Later, after his mother divorced his father and married Bill Broeksmit, he was adopted by Broeksmit when he was 9. [[Adam Schiff]] subpoenaed Val Broeksmit for the Broeksmit materials about Deutsche Bank to begin House of Representatives investigations into Trump and Deutsche Bank.<ref name="Me and My Whistle-Blower"/><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Flitter |first1=Emily |last2=Enrich |first2=David |date=15 April 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank Is Subpoenaed for Trump Records by House Democrats |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/15/business/deutsche-bank-trump-finances-congress.html |access-date=27 May 2026 |issn=0362-4331}}Deutsche Bank Is Subpoenaed for Trump Records by House Democrats</ref>}}

===Libor scandal, 2015===
{{see also|Libor scandal}}
On 23 April 2015, Deutsche Bank agreed to a combined US$2.5&nbsp;billion in fines – a US$2.175&nbsp;billion fine by American regulators, and a €227&nbsp;million penalty by British authorities – for its involvement in the [[Libor scandal]] uncovered in June 2012. It was one of several banks colluding to fix interest rates used to price hundreds of trillions of dollars of loans and contracts worldwide, including mortgages and student loans.<ref name="NYTFine" /><ref name="ReutersFine" /> Deutsche Bank also pleaded guilty to wire fraud, acknowledging that at least 29 employees had engaged in illegal activity. It was required to dismiss all employees who were involved with the fraudulent transactions.<ref name=ReutersFine /> However, no individuals were charged with criminal wrongdoing. In a Libor first, Deutsche Bank will be required to install an independent monitor.<ref name="NYTFine">{{cite news |last1=Protess |first1=Ben |last2=Ewing |first2=Jack |date=23 April 2015 |title=Deutsche Bank to Pay $2.5 Billion Fine to Settle Rate-Rigging Case |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/04/24/business/dealbook/deutsche-bank-settlement-rates.html?ref=dealbook |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> Commenting on the fine, Britain's [[Financial Conduct Authority]] director Georgina Philippou said "This case stands out for the seriousness and duration of the breaches&nbsp;... One division at Deutsche Bank had a culture of generating profits without proper regard to the integrity of the market. This wasn't limited to a few individuals but, on certain desks, it appeared deeply ingrained."<ref name="ReutersFine">{{cite news |last1=Ridley |first1=Kirstin |last2=Freifeld |first2=Karen |date=23 April 2015 |title=Deutsche Bank fined record $2.5 billion in rate rigging inquiry |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-deutschebank-libor-settlement-idUSKBN0NE12U20150423 |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> The fine represented a record for interest rate related cases, eclipsing a $1.5&nbsp;billion Libor related fine to UBS, and the then-record $450&nbsp;million fine assessed to Barclays earlier in the case.<ref name=ReutersFine /><ref name=NYTFine /> The size of the fine reflected the breadth of wrongdoing at Deutsche Bank, the bank's poor oversight of traders, and its failure to take action when it uncovered signs of abuse internally.<ref name=NYTFine />

=== U.S. sanctions violations, 2015 ===
On 5 November 2015, Deutsche Bank was ordered to pay US$258&nbsp;million (€237.2&nbsp;million) in penalties imposed by the [[New York State Department of Financial Services]] (NYDFS) and the [[United States Federal Reserve Bank]] after the bank was caught doing business with Burma, Libya, Sudan, Iran, and Syria, which were under [[US sanctions]] at the time. According to the US federal authorities, Deutsche Bank handled 27,200 US dollar clearing transactions valued at more than US$10.86&nbsp;billion (€9.98&nbsp;billion) to help evade US sanctions between early 1999 until 2006 which were done on behalf of Iranian, Libyan, Syrian, Burmese, and Sudanese financial institutions and other entities subject to US sanctions, including entities on the [[Specially Designated Nationals]] by the [[Office of Foreign Assets Control]].<ref>{{cite news |date=4 November 2015 |title=Deutsche Bank to pay US$258m for violating US sanctions |url=http://www.channelnewsasia.com/news/business/deutsche-bank-to-pay-us/2239426.html |work=[[Channel NewsAsia]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160106211442/http://www.channelnewsasia.com/news/business/deutsche-bank-tu-pay-us/2239426.html |archive-date=6 January 2016 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=4 November 2015 |title=NYDFS Announces Deutsche Bank to Pay $258 Million, Install Independent Monitor, Terminate Employees for Transactions on Behalf of Iran, Syria, Sudan, Other Sanctioned Entities |url=http://www.dfs.ny.gov/about/press/pr1511041.htm |work=[[New York State Department of Financial Services|NYDFS]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151107170649/http://www.dfs.ny.gov/about/press/pr1511041.htm |archive-date=7 November 2015 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> In response to the penalties, the bank will pay US$200&nbsp;million (€184&nbsp;million) to the NYDFS while the rest (US$58&nbsp;million; €53.3&nbsp;million) will go to the [[Federal Reserve]]. In addition to the payment, the bank will install an independent monitor, fire six employees who were involved in the incident, and ban three other employees from any work involving the bank's US-based operations.<ref>{{cite news |last=Press Association |date=4 November 2015 |title=Deutsche Bank fined $258m for violating US sanctions |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2015/nov/04/deutsche-bank-us-sanctions-fine |work=[[The Guardian]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref>

===Tax evasion, 2016===
In June 2016 six former employees in Germany were accused of being involved in a major tax fraud deal with CO<sub>2</sub> emission certificates, and most of them were subsequently convicted. It was estimated that the sum of money in the tax evasion scandal might have been as high as €850&nbsp;million. Deutsche Bank itself was not convicted due to an absence of corporate liability laws in Germany.<ref>{{cite news |date=13 June 2016 |title=Steuerbetrug: Sechs Ex-Mitarbeiter der Deutschen Bank verurteilt |url=https://www.zeit.de/wirtschaft/unternehmen/2016-06/steuerbetrug-deutsche-bank-umsatzsteuer-landgericht-frankfurt |work=[[Die Zeit]] |language=de |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref>

===Russian money-laundering operations, 1990s −2017===
In January 2017, the bank was fined $425&nbsp;million by the [[New York State Department of Financial Services]] (DFS)<ref>{{cite news |date=30 January 2017 |title=DFS Fines Deutsche Bank $425 Million for Russian Mirror-Trading Scheme |url=http://www.dfs.ny.gov/about/press/pr1701301.htm |work=[[New York State Department of Financial Services|NYDFS]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216204336/http://www.dfs.ny.gov/about/press/pr1701301.htm |archive-date=16 February 2017 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> and £163&nbsp;million by the UK [[Financial Conduct Authority]]<ref>{{cite news |last=Treanor |first=Jill |date=31 January 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank fined $630m over Russia money laundering claims |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2017/jan/31/deutsche-bank-fined-630m-over-russia-money-laundering-claims |work=[[The Guardian]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> regarding accusations of [[money laundering|laundering]] $10&nbsp;billion out of Russia.<ref>{{cite news |last=Thomas |first=Landon Jr. |date=30 January 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank Fined for Helping Russians Launder $10 Billion |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/01/30/business/dealbook/deutsche-bank-fined-for-helping-russians-launder-10-billion.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Wang |first=Christine |date=30 January 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank to pay $425 million fine over Russian money-laundering scheme: New York regulator |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2017/01/30/deutsche-bank-to-pay-425-million-fine-over-russian-money-laundering.html |work=[[CNBC]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170131001021/http://www.cnbc.com/2017/01/30/deutsche-bank-to-pay-425-million-fine-over-russian-money-laundering.html |archive-date=31 January 2017 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Farrell |first1=Greg |last2=Geiger |first2=Keri |date=31 January 2017 |title=Deutsche Bank Ends N.Y. Mirror-Trade Probe for $425 Million |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-01-30/deutsche-bank-settles-n-y-mirror-trade-probe-for-425-million |work=[[Bloomberg L.P.|Bloomberg]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> In the decade preceding the Russian mirror-trading scheme, Deutsche Bank was informed of substantial and widespread compliance concerns. The offsetting trades in this instance lacked economic purpose and could have been used to facilitate money laundering or other illegal activity.<ref>{{cite news |date=30 January 2017 |title=Consent Order Under New York Banking Law §§ 39, 44 and 44-a |url=https://www.dfs.ny.gov/system/files/documents/2020/03/ea170130_deustche_bank.pdf |work=[[New York State Department of Financial Services|NYDFS]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210819120502/https://www.dfs.ny.gov/system/files/documents/2020/03/ea170130_deustche_bank.pdf |archive-date=19 August 2021 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> On 30 January 2017, the [[New York State Department of Financial Services|NYSDFS (New York State Department of Financial Services)]] fined Deutsche Bank $425&nbsp;million for violating New York's [[Money laundering#Anti-money laundering|anti-money laundering laws]]. There was a "mirror trading" scheme involved. Deutsche Bank's Moscow, London, and New York branches laundered $10&nbsp;billion out of Russia.<ref name="Stewart-2019" />

The Global Laundromat scandal revealed Deutsche Bank's involvement in a vast money-laundering operation over the period 2010–2014. The operation may have involved as much as $80&nbsp;billion. In 2019, ''The Guardian'' reported that a confidential internal report at Deutsche Bank showed that the bank could face fines, legal action, and even possible prosecution of senior management over the bank's role in the money laundering.<ref>{{cite news |last=Harding |first=Luke |date=17 April 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank faces action over $20bn Russian money-laundering scheme |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/apr/17/deutsche-bank-faces-action-over-20bn-russian-money-laundering-scheme |work=[[The Guardian]] |url-access=subscription |language=en |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In 2020, it was reported that Deutsche Bank was pursuing an expansion of its Russia operations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kowsmann |first1=Patricia |last2=Strasburg |first2=Jenny |date=26 November 2020 |title=Outside Monitors Urge Deutsche Bank to Quit Russia |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/outside-monitors-urge-deutsche-bank-to-quit-russia-11606386601 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In the wake of [[2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine|Russia's 2022 invasion of Ukraine]], Deutsche Bank refused to close down its Russia business. At the same time, other banks and major businesses were exiting Russia.<ref>{{cite news |last=Gilchrist |first=Karen |date=10 March 2022 |title=Deutsche Bank defends decision not to exit Russia: It's not 'practical' right now |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/03/10/deutsche-bank-says-not-practical-to-exit-russia-business.html |work=[[CNBC]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In June 2023, the bank notified customers that it could no longer guarantee them access to the shares they hold on the basis of depositary receipts issued prior to February 2022. He explained this by the shortage of shares in the Russian depository. The bank also warned that it would be able to return the funds for the share significantly below the market price.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cruise |first1=Sinead |last2=Mandl |first2=Carolina |date=26 June 2023 |title=Exclusive: Deutsche Bank tells investors some of their Russian shares are missing |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/finance/deutsche-bank-tells-investors-some-their-russian-shares-are-missing/ |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Fine for business with Jeffrey Epstein, 2020 ===
For decades, Deutsche Bank lent money and traded currencies for multimillionaire [[Jeffrey Epstein]] up to May 2019, long after his 2008 [[guilty plea]] in Florida to soliciting prostitution from underage girls, according to news reports.<ref>{{cite tweet |user=davidenrich |date=10 July 2019 |number=1149116131250266112 |title=Exclusive: @DeutscheBank had an extensive relationship with Jeffrey Epstein... |url=https://twitter.com/davidenrich/status/1149116131250266112 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="Stewart-2019">{{cite news |last1=Stewart |first1=James B. |last2=Goldstein |first2=Matthew |last3=Kelly |first3=Kate |last4=Enrich |first4=David |date=10 July 2019 |title=Jeffrey Epstein’s Fortune May Be More Illusion Than Fact |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/10/business/jeffrey-epstein-net-worth.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Metcalf |first1=Tom |last2=Robinson |first2=Matt |date=10 July 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank ended its relationship with Jeffrey Epstein this year |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/news/nation/2019/07/10/deutsche-bank-ended-its-relationship-with-jeffrey-epstein-this-year/ysdWK3tkvmj4XGFpkn1c6O/story.html |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> Epstein and his businesses had dozens of accounts through the private-banking division.<ref name="Enrich-2019a">{{cite news |last1=Enrich |first1=David |last2=Becker |first2=Jo |date=23 July 2019 |title=Jeffrey Epstein Moved Money Overseas in Transactions His Bank Flagged to U.S. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/23/business/jeffrey-epstein-deutsche-bank.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Strasburg |first1=Jenny |last2=Hong |first2=Nicole |date=23 July 2019 |title=Jeffrey Epstein's Financial Trail Goes Through Deutsche Bank |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/jeffrey-epsteins-financial-trail-goes-through-deutsche-bank-11563911131 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> From 2013 to 2018, "Epstein, his related entities and his associates" had opened over forty accounts with Deutsche Bank.<ref name="NYT_20200707" /> This occurred after JPMorgan had stopped doing business with Epstein in 2013.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Robert |last2=Aliaj |first2=Ortenca |last3=Mourselas |first3=Costas |last4=Foy |first4=Simon |last5=Müller |first5=Florian |date=25 February 2026 |title=How Deutsche Bank rolled out the red carpet for Jeffrey Epstein |url=https://www.ft.com/content/b1d9e8c4-1874-4c82-941d-9a2e1512bc4c |work=[[Financial Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

According to ''The New York Times'', Deutsche Bank managers overruled compliance officers who raised concerns about Epstein's reputation''.''<ref name="Stewart-2019" /> The bank found suspicious transactions in which Epstein moved money out of the United States, ''[[The Times]]'' reported.<ref name="Enrich-2019a" /> In July 2020, the [[New York Department of Financial Services]] (DFS) imposed a $150&nbsp;million penalty on Deutsche Bank, in connection with Epstein. The bank had "ignored red flags on Epstein".<ref name="NYT_20200707">{{cite news |last=Goldstein |first=Matthew |date=7 July 2020 |title=Deutsche Bank Settles Over Ignored Red Flags on Jeffrey Epstein |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/10/business/jeffrey-epstein-net-worth.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="DFS_PR_20200707">{{cite press release |title=Superintendent Lacewell Announces DFS Imposes $150 Million Penalty on Deutsche Bank in Connection with Bank's Relationship with Jeffrey Epstein and Correspondent Relationships with Danske Estonia And FBME Bank |date=7 July 2020 |url=https://www.dfs.ny.gov/reports_and_publications/press_releases/pr202007071 |work=[[New York State Department of Financial Services|Department of Financial Services]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> Later that month, the son and husband of the judge involved in the trial were shot by a gunman posing as a FedEx driver, with the son dying of his injuries.<ref>{{cite news |last=Roberts |first=Jeff John |date=20 July 2020 |title=Hitman kills son of judge overseeing Deutsche Bank-Epstein trial |url=https://fortune.com/2020/07/20/epstein-trial-judges-son-killed-hitman-deutsche-bank-esther-salas/ |work=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In November 2022, two unnamed women who had accused Epstein of sexual abuse and sex trafficking sued Deutsche Bank for its role in enabling Epstein to run his sex-trafficking operations by ignoring red flags regarding his account and the withdrawal of suspiciously high sums of money.<ref>{{cite news |last=Jones |first=Lora |date=25 November 2022 |title=Jeffrey Epstein victims sue several major banks |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/business-63750504 |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Involvement in Danske Bank money-laundering scandal, 2018 ===
On 19 November 2018, a whistleblower of the [[Danske Bank money laundering scandal]] stated that a large European bank was involved in helping Danske process $150&nbsp;billion in suspect funds.<ref name="Reuters2018-11-19">{{cite news |last1=Jensen |first1=Teis |last2=Gronholt-Pedersen |first2=Jacob |date=19 November 2018 |title=Danske whistleblower says big European bank handled $150 billion in payments |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-danske-bank-moneylaundering/danske-whistleblower-says-big-european-bank-handled-150-billion-in-payments-idUSKCN1NO0ZR |work=[[Reuters]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181217002110/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-danske-bank-moneylaundering/danske-whistleblower-says-big-european-bank-handled-150-billion-in-payments-idUSKCN1NO0ZR |archive-date=17 December 2018 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> Although the whistleblower, [[Howard Wilkinson (whistleblower)|Howard Wilkinson]], did not name Deutsche Bank directly, another inside source claimed the institute in question was Deutsche Bank's U.S. unit.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Arons |first1=Steven |last2=Barnert |first2=Jan-Patrick |last3=Comfort |first3=Nicholas |date=20 November 2018 |title=Deutsche Bank Hits Record Low on New Worry Over Danske Role |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-11-20/deutsche-bank-hits-another-record-low-on-money-laundering-worry |website=[[Bloomberg L.P.|Bloomberg]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129162444/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-11-20/deutsche-bank-hits-another-record-low-on-money-laundering-worry |archive-date=29 November 2018 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>
In 2020 it became known that the U.S. arm of Deutsche Bank processed more than $150&nbsp;billion of the $230&nbsp;billion dirty money through New York, for which it was fined 150 million $. After a raid in 2019, Frankfurt-based prosecutors imposed a fine of $15.8&nbsp;million in 2020 for DB's failure on more than 600 occasions to promptly report suspicious transactions.<ref name="icij">{{cite news |last=Bowers |first=Simon |date=16 October 2020 |title=German prosecutors drop probe into Deutsche Bank ties to Estonian dirty money scandal |url=https://www.icij.org/investigations/fincen-files/german-prosecutors-drop-probe-into-deutsche-bank-ties-to-estonian-dirty-money-scandal/ |work=International Consortium of Investigative Journalists |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

===Improper handling of ADRs, 2018===

On 20 July 2018, Deutsche Bank agreed to pay nearly $75&nbsp;million to settle charges of improper handling of "pre-released" [[American depositary receipt]] (ADRs) under investigation of the [[U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission]] (SEC). Deutsche Bank didn't admit or deny the investigation findings but agreed to pay disgorgement of more than $44.4&nbsp;million in ill-gotten gains plus $6.6&nbsp;million in prejudgment interest and a penalty of $22.2&nbsp;million.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Deutsche Bank to Pay Nearly $75 Million for Improper Handling of ADRs |url=https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2018-138 |work=[[U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission|SEC]] |date=13 July 2018 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Katanga |date=20 July 2018 |title=Deutsche to pay $75 million to settle ADRs abuses case, U.S. SEC says |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-sec-idUSKBN1KA274 |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Malaysian 1MDB fund ===
In July 2019, U.S. prosecutors investigated Deutsche Bank's role in a multibillion-dollar fraud scandal involving the [[1Malaysia Development Berhad]], or 1MDB.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Viswanatha |first1=Aruna |last2=Hope |first2=Bradley |last3=Strasburg |first3=Jenny |date=10 July 2019 |title=U.S. Investigating Deutsche Bank's Dealings With Malaysian Fund 1MDB |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-investigating-deutsche-bank-s-dealings-with-malaysian-fund-1mdb-11562798907 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="NYTMalaysia">{{cite news |last=Flitter |first=Emily |date=10 July 2019 |title=Deutsche Bank Caught Up in Scandal Over Malaysian 1MDB Fund |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/10/business/deutsche-bank-1mdb-malaysia.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> Deutsche Bank helped raise $1.2&nbsp;billion for the 1MDB in 2014.<ref name="NYTMalaysia" /> As of May 2021 Malaysia sued Deutsche Bank to recover billions in alleged losses from a corruption scandal at the fund.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 May 2021 |title=Malaysia sues Deutsche Bank, JP Morgan, Coutts over 1MDB |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/malaysia-sues-deutsche-bank-jp-morgan-coutts-over-1mdb-2021-05-10/ |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Commodities trading, bribery fine, 2021 ===
In January 2021, Deutsche Bank agreed to pay a U.S. fine of more than $130&nbsp;million for a scheme to conceal bribes to foreign officials in countries such as [[Saudi Arabia]] and China, and the city of [[Abu Dhabi]], between 2008 and 2017 and a commodities case where it [[Spoofing (finance)|spoofed]] precious metals futures.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wolf |first=Brett |date=13 January 2021 |title=Deutsche Bank reaches $130 million settlement over U.S. bribery, commodities |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/bc-finreg-deutsche-bribery-settlement-idUSKBN29I2VF |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=Deutsche Bank Agrees to Pay over $130 Million to Resolve Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and Fraud Case |url=https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/deutsche-bank-agrees-pay-over-130-million-resolve-foreign-corrupt-practices-act-and-fraud |work=[[U.S. Department of Justice|Department of Justice]] |date=8 January 2021 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Deutsche Welle |date=8 January 2021 |title=Deutsche Bank handed $124 million in bribery fines by US court |url=https://www.dw.com/en/deutsche-bank-handed-124-million-in-bribery-fines-by-us-court/a-56176485 |work=[[Deutsche Welle|DW]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Strip club scandal, 2022 ===
In March 2022, Ben Darsney, Ravi Raghunathan, Brandon Sun, and Daniel Gaona were exposed for trying to list strip club nights out as legitimate business visit expenses. Brandon Sun attempted to cover up the incident, but the bankers were let go for violating the Company Code of Conduct.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tan |first1=Gillian |last2=Arons |first2=Steven |date=25 March 2022 |title=Deutsche Bank Fired Senior Bankers over Strip Club Bill |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-03-25/deutsche-bank-fired-senior-bankers-over-expensed-strip-club-bill |work=[[Bloomberg L.P.|Bloomberg]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Greenwashing, 2022 ===
On 31 May 2022, police in Germany raided the offices of Deutsche Bank in Frankfurt over allegations of greenwashing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Arons |first1=Steven |last2=Matussek |first2=Karin |last3=Comfort |first3=Nicholas |date=31 May 2022 |title=Deutsche Bank, DWS Raided Over Allegations of Greenwashing |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-05-31/deutsche-bank-s-dws-unit-raided-amid-allegations-of-greenwashing |work=[[Bloomberg L.P.|Bloomberg]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In late July 2023, the Financial Times reported that DWS (80% owned by Deutsche Bank) which was also involved in the case was nearing a settlement having earmarked €21mn for the settlement and incurred €39mn in legal costs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Storbeck |first1=Olaf |date=26 July 2023 |title=DWS closes in on settlement over greenwashing scandal |url=https://www.ft.com/content/38be4231-21cf-46e3-8b6a-88a4e923f01c |work=[[Financial Times]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> DWS had made misleading statements about the size of its ESG assets and the employee who raised concerns was fired unfairly. Reuters composed a more extensive timeline highlighting how the issue developed over the course of several years including when the stock price fell sharply on news of a US SEC investigation and the resignation of the CEO.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 July 2023 |title=Deutsche Bank's DWS and allegations of 'greenwashing' |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/finance/deutsche-banks-dws-allegations-greenwashing-2022-06-09/ |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

=== Anti-competitive Bond Trading, 2005–2016 ===
In 2023, it was revealed that Deutsche Bank was involved in anti-competitive bond trading in UK and EU bonds. In Europe, Deutsche revealed cartel activities in the period between 2005 and 2016 to regulators to receive immunity. "Deutsche Bank would have been fined almost €156 million for its participation in the absence of immunity."<ref>{{cite web |date=6 December 2023 |title=Competition & Regulatory Newsletter: European Commission fines Rabobank over bonds trading cartel |url=https://www.slaughterandmay.com/insights/importedcontent/competition-regulatory-newsletter-european-commission-fines-rabobank-over-bonds-trading-cartel/ |work=[[Slaughter and May]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref> In the UK, Deutsche also admitted to wrongdoing for the period 2009–2013 to the Competition and Markets Authority.<ref>{{cite news |date=24 May 2023 |title=Deutsche Bank and Citi admit anti-competitive activity in UK bond market |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/24/investing/deutsche-citi-antitrust-uk-bonds |work=[[CNN Business]] |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

== Leadership ==
{{Chief executives of Deutsche Bank}}
After Deutsche Bank was first organized in 1870, the Management Board was represented by a Speaker ({{langx|de|Vorstandssprecher}}). Beginning in February 2012, the bank has been led by two co-CEOs; in July 2015 it announced it would be led by one CEO beginning in 2016.<ref>{{cite web |date=2012 |title=Deutsche Bank Annual Report |url=https://annualreport.deutsche-bank.com/2012/ar/deutschebankgroup/corporateprofile/managementstructure.html |work=Deutsche Bank |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304023637/https://annualreport.deutsche-bank.com/2012/ar/deutschebankgroup/corporateprofile/managementstructure.html |archive-date=4 March 2016 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

